# Project instructions

- This is a freelance order run by the partjob workflow (`~/.agents/skills/partjob/SKILL.md`; JC invokes it as `/partjob`). Work on the order follows its rules even when JC did not type the command.
- Read `.gig/JOB.md` before working. Price and payment facts come only from `.gig/QUOTE.md`; quotes in old reports are void.
- Original materials are read-only{% if material_path %}: `{{ material_path }}`{% endif %}. Copy needed files to `data/`; client data is never committed.
- New results go to new directories, never overwriting old ones; in project records failures and unusable states stay visible.
- Never tune numbers toward the client's expectations or a paper. Reported figures come from saved results, with the data split, configuration, seeds and aggregation stated in the project records.
- At the end of every phase append to "Status" in `.gig/JOB.md`: commands, verification figures, commit, remaining work, whether anything was sent out.
- Temporary files and recovery copies go in `.scratch/`, cleared at the end of a phase. Create nothing in the parent directory.
- Actions that need JC's explicit approval: sending anything out, pushing to a remote, deleting or archiving, paid remote resources (paid databases included), scope changes beyond the confirmed decisions, edits to `.gig/QUOTE.md`.
- ASCII punctuation in project records; the manuscript follows its target journal. English directory and file names. Explain concepts with concrete names, no undefined abbreviations.
- Commit each verified unit of work locally; pushing needs separate authorisation.

## Writing projects

- The manuscript is the client's text. Its terms come only from the manuscript itself and the cited literature. CONTEXT.md, ADRs, reports and JOB.md are process records: words coined there never enter the manuscript.
- Results in the manuscript follow the field's convention: held-out test results, mean and standard deviation over seeds, no per-seed discussion, only the variants JC chose. Validation-only comparisons stay in the project records.
- Before polishing a headline claim, check that it rests on a held-out split. When it does not, raise it to JC as a decision instead of polishing it.
- No fabricated data, references or citations. Every citation resolves to a paper that exists and says what the text claims.
- When the client gave no concrete requirement, grill before any literature search or topic work (`/partjob start`, step 7).
- Literature: look in JC's Zotero library first. Downloads go to `references/papers/<year>-<journal>-<first-author>.pdf` (gitignored) and into Zotero; extracted text goes to `.scratch/refs/` and is reused.
- LaTeX builds go to `.scratch/build/`, never the output directory JC's editor compiles into; check for a running `latexmk -pvc` first. Build once per batch of edits.
- Word counts come from one script kept in the project (`scripts/wordcount.py`), reused for every count.
- Preview: watermarked results pages (abstract, key tables and figures). The full delivery goes through `/partjob pack` and `/partjob send` as a full package.

